  "notes": "Traditional-leaning Alabama country — baritone drawl, steel and acoustic, narrative lyrics, minimal programming. The key concentration is documented: \"D♭ major and F♯ major recur across his catalogue alongside B/D/E,\" which is a flatter and sharper set than the open-guitar keys the 90s lane uses, and the ballads are notated \"~64–77 with the backbeat on 2&4\" with the recurring 142/152 readings being doubled values of ~71/76. Drums are deliberately plain: \"cross-stick verses, snare from the chorus, kick on 1 and 3, tambourine on chorus 8ths, minimal programming beyond a clap under the snare on radio mixes.\" Harmony is \"I–IV–V and I–V–vi–IV with V7 honky-tonk colour,\" so the seventh share carries the dominant and the numerals stay degree-only. The bass is stated with a negative that matters: \"live electric bass, root-fifth, long notes; no sub on the core catalogue,\" so no `drums.bass808` block is authored at all. Melody is \"low baritone drawl, narrow range, long note values, near-spoken verses,\" and the entry adds that \"the duet cuts hand a full section to a female lead,\" which is why the counter runs at a high answer density rather than as a pad. The form is called \"deliberately old-fashioned … with no post-chorus hooks.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
